Art choices 

Drama 
• Develop the student's creativity; 
• Work on intonation, gestures and facial expressions; 
• Have a taste for working in a team and surpassing oneself; 
• Interpretation scenes (comedic and dramatic) and choir work; 
• Become more comfortable speaking in public, communicating and controlling one's stress; 
• Different learning situations: directing, playwriting, playwriting, filming advertisements, directed improvisation, designing outlines and building characters; 

• Exam in front of an audience 
• Open to all: intermediate level 
• Possibility of a preparatory meeting for new students. 

Visual arts 
• Open to all: beginners, intermediate or advanced (and new students). 

• Disciplines and themes explored: design principles, observational drawing, still life, landscape art, bas-relief art, collage art, artistic movements that have marked the history of art, great painters, acrylic painting, media art and Google Doddles; • Skills assessed: Create personal or media images (creative projects, technical exercises and explorations with various mediums) and Appreciate works of art (appreciation exams, various appreciations, written reflections (creative process), sharing and discussions; 

• The creative projects will allow the student to develop their artistic skills, their own visual language and their authenticity in addition to stimulating their creativity and imagination; 

• The appreciation projects will allow the student to develop their attention, their sense of observation, their sense of appreciation and their critical judgment; 

• Room for personal exploration, creativity and experimentation (creative project and sketchbook); Page Number: 9 
9 

Dance 
• Develop creativity, coordination, rhythm, confidence and autonomy; 

• Learn various basic dance techniques; 

• Work on interpreting the sequences and choreographies learned in class; 

• Creative work based on different themes and styles of dance (project on the decades, project on urban dance and project on contemporary dance, project on childhood memories) 

• Presentation of the projects in front of the students in the class and in front of an audience on stage; 

• Analysis and comparison of different choreographic works; 

• Research work on a dance style; 

• Dance theory (anatomy of the human body); 

• Open to all: beginners, intermediate or advanced. 

Guitar 
• Deepen your knowledge of the instrument and improve your mastery of the guitar; 

• Exploration of the solo guitar repertoire, in duo and other formations in different musical styles;

• Develop autonomy in learning musical pieces in order to work on musical projects
that can better respect the musical abilities and interests of each student;

• Deepen theoretical knowledge;

• Exploration of film music composition techniques and introduction to the concept of musical criticism. Page Number: 10 
Course choices - Groups 302-304-306 
Course Description Additional information 
Computer science 

Concepts covered: 
• Introduction to programming: Microbit programming 
• Programmer's computing: HTML, CSS and JavaScript 
• Game computing: video game programming 
• Artist's computing: video editing 
• Computing by a 4th secondary student: working by project and learning by solving problems 

Qualities sought: 
• Autonomy 
• Logical mind 
• Organization 
• Curiosity 

The different projects: 
• Block programming using mini-
computers called Microbit 

• Use of spreadsheets 

• Creation of video games (HTML, CSS and Java Script) 
 Memory game 
 Pong 
 Flappy Bird 

• Stop-motion video editing

Entrepreneurship
Be different! Be an entrepreneur!
Do you need something concrete? Come take a course focused on
projects!

Concepts covered:
An entrepreneur, the company, human resources, marketing,
advertising, finance, the market, a business plan, mentoring, management and production

Qualities sought:
• Desire for achievement and taking on challenges
• Innovation
• Ability to work in a team
• Perseverance
• Leadership

The different projects:

• Christmas market: design of a product to
sell

• “The Dragons” project: design of a business plan

• Creation of escape rooms

• Interview with an entrepreneur

10 Page Number: 11 
11 

Introduction to 
Psychology 

The introductory course to psychology allows you to explore an 
extraordinary subject, the human being, in all the complexity of his nature! 

Notions covered: 
The tasks of the psychologist, schools of thought and sectors of 
practice, the formation and transformation of identity, 
personality and its disorders (borderline, obsessive-compulsive, 
narcissistic, paranoid, etc.), positive psychology (scientific 
study of well-being), eating disorders (anorexia, 
bulimia, etc.), consumer psychology (advertising and 
marketing), neurological, psychological and psychiatric syndromes 
(Down, Asperger, Tourette, Münchhausen, etc.), 
research in human sciences, etc. 

Qualities sought: 
• Open-mindedness 
• Curiosity and intellectual rigor 
• Interest in humans 
• Hard-working 
• This interactive course is interspersed with discussions and 
theoretical notions. The student is led to 
understand and master knowledge and to use it in his daily life. 

• This course is a gateway to the vast 
universe of human sciences and rigorously prepares for college courses in 
psychology and human sciences. 

• This course is evaluated by theoretical exams 
and a team research project. 

This course is designed so that students who 
wish to do so can also enroll in the 5th year of secondary 
psychology course to continue their learning on different subjects. Page Number: 12 
12 
